> >In a PPL system, who defines and protects intellectual property?  If
> >the PPL over there refuses to respect the patent I got from the PPL
> >over here, how do I get my just rights respected?
> 
> I doubt patents will exist. They make very little sense, anyway.
> Copyrights one can imagine arising out of contracts associated with
> the purchase of a book -- i.e. if you want to buy my book, you have to
> agree not to copy it. However, under what libertarian theory can one
> imagine gaining a right to tell someone who independantly invents a
> widget you have invented that he can't use his completely
> independantly derived discovery?

Part of the original reasoning behind patents was that they would allow secret
techniques to eventually be used by everyone, but with an incentive to the 
inventor to allow this. The inventor reveals his secret, but for a limited time
he is able to make even more money from it (by selling licences, and by not 
having to worry about secrecy, increasing his freedom to expand).
	Of course, the intricacies of independent reinvention are not covered
by this.
	The system has now evolved (or perhaps decayed) to a point where 
perpetual copyrights are quite possible, with some legal inventiveness, 
particularly in the US, the home of legal inventiveness. The same is becoming 
true of copyrights.

	SO, given all this, how do people feel about the LPF? 
> 
> Perry
