Date: Wed, 27 Jan 93 1:25:34 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ANARCHY: The Twilight of Intellectual Property?

The Twilight of Intellectual Property?


Hal Finney makes some spirited arguments in favor of intellectual
property rights:

> Let me offer another argument in favor of intellectual property rights.
> I will make an analogy with ordinary property rights.
> 
> Most of us agree that there are many beneficial effects of observing
> property rights.  Some favor them on purely ethical grounds, but I want
> to focus on the pragmatic arguments.  One of the advantages of property
> rights is that they reward people for working to develop their property.
> If I make an improvement in something I own - build a hotel on my land,
> say - I reap the benefit of that investment.  In a system without
> property rights, anyone might be able to do what they want with my hotel
> or my land.  I would have little incentive to improve it.
.......
> I am suggesting here that this argument applies with equal force to
> intellectual property.  I'm not saying that intellectual property is
> exactly like other forms of property; I recognize that intellectual
> property is "inalienable", that is, you don't lose it if you give it to
> someone else.  But the general principle is that people will be motivated to
> work harder if they control what they have created than if the results of
> their efforts become public property.

What Hal is ignoring is the issue of _enforceability_. Land ownership,
whatever its merits, whatever its faults, is generally enforceable by
various combinations of PPL arrangements, mutual defense arrangements,
government actions, and physical barriers (locks, fences, alarm
systems, etc.).

Intellectual property, in various forms, has typically required a
coercive state. In fact, licensing of professionals, professional
guilds, and othe distortions of free market behavior are prime
examples of state coercion. The granting of monopolies on techniques
and knowleges is a tool of the state.

Consider medieval guilds. Until the arrival of cheap printing, the
"intellectual property" of medieval guilds was adequately defended by
various combinations of punishment by the King, penalties by local
sheriffs, ostracism by other guilds (sort of the way unions recognize
each others strikes), and the general difficulties in disseminating
knowledge. Before books were widely available, something as relatively
straightforward as tanning leather was a closely held secret,
requiring years of apprenticeship. No doubt the rulers liked this
situation, as it made collection of taxes and regulation of commerce
much easier. And it gave the crown another lever to use.

As books and other forms of learning spread, the power of the guilds
declined. No doubt there were apologists for the idea that knowledge
could be owned--"But if _anyone_ can become a blacksmith, how will the
investments blacksmiths make in training ever pay off?"

(And obviously this form of intellectual property idea permeates our
society today, especially with the various professional
guilds--lawyers, doctors, CPAs, engineers, etc.--and the licensing and
credentialling trends in some many areas.)

Now I'm not taking a moral stand here (though my sympathies should be
obvious). What's important is that guilds collapsed largely because
the knowledge they carried could no longer be bottled up. "How To"
books were the most popular of the early books, after Bibles and
hymnals. Perhaps there were attempts to suppress them...in any case,
the genie got out of the bottle.

Fast forward to today---music and video is barely protectable,
pirating is commonplace, nearly everyone uses Xerox machines to copy
whatever they want, bootleg software is passed around, and on and on.
The courts are choking with lawsuits, countersuits, and chaos.

And newer technology (I mentioned scanners, OCR software, and
anonymous remailers as one example) is making the protection of
intellectual property even more tenuous. Unlike land rights, which
seem fairly defendable (practically, physically), many types of modern
"intellectual property" have no reasonable defense method short of
using various Big Brother techniques: raids on companies and houses
(as the Software Publishers Association has done in league with local
authorities, merely by _suspecting_ that illegal software copies exist
at a site), surveillance, networks of informants, civil forfeiture
penalties, and so on. And still it fails.

Technological solutions may help. For example, digital signatures can
be used to "sign" a physical object, to prevent forgeries. (Details:
"Light Signatures," an LA company, provided a system for
Harley-Davidson to eliminate spare parts forgeries. A scan of exposed
metal resulted in a waveform of varying light intensities. This
sequence of bits was then encrypted at the factory with the private
key of the factory and the number was stamped on the part. A dealer
could confirm the part was made by Harley by taking the stamped
number, applying Harley's _public key_ to it, and comparing the
resulting sequence to the waveform he could measure. No forger would
be able to generate the stamped number, as he lacked the private key.
But any dealer could quickly check the "fingerprint.")

Long term, nearly anything that can be expressed in terms of bits will
be essentially unprotectable, just as so many things are already
beyond the bounds of practical protection (ideas, styles, and
influences). 

In many areas, such as in our homes and amongst friends, the battle
has already largely been lost. CDs are copied, movies are taped, and
books are Xeroxed. (Notice that CD rentals are now illegal in most
places, though libraries have mostly escaped this move, so far. How
long before _books_ fall under this kind of law, however futile in the
long run?)

Now I'm not arguing that contracts cannot be signed between seller and
buyer--this is a "solution" touted by several in this thread. Fine,
for those who stick to the terms. But an essential part of any PPL
scheme must be _detection_ of violations and _enforcement_ of claims.
How much will insurance cost to pay for enforcement costs against
those who are somehow caught with an illegal copy? 

Practically speaking, the chances of getting caught are low,
punishment is likely to be light--especially if a person claims to
have been unaware there was any kind of retriction on the material ("I
bought it at the flea market, and there wasn't anything saying it was
copyrighted!"), so the potential profits for PPL agencies are not too
tempting. Not at all like protecting clients from physical assaults
and robberies. I suspect Benson would express this in terms of costs
of bringing charges...only a coercive state and court system that
subsidizes costs would allow such "intellectual property scofflaws" to
be caught, prosecuted, and punished.

The newer technologies make both detection and enforcement even more
difficult. "Crypto anarchy" will make information propagation much
easier (and let me note for the readers of this list who don't follow
the "Cypherpunks" list that Hal is himself a very active contributor
to the universe of anonymous remailers. Hoist by his own petard, so to
speak!)

I have more to say, but I'll end with more of Hal's posting and then
one from own writing which has some echoes, and contrasts, with Hal's
thoughts. 

> An unspoiled, virgin continent lies before you.  If you'll let people put
> up fences, acquire ownership rights in the property they develop, you'll
> have vigorous, healthy expansion into new areas.  But if you keep the
> area common, let any person do whatever he wants with the improvements
> created by another, you're going to have much weaker growth.

Ironically, consider some of the perhaps too flowery words from my
"Crypt Anarchist Manifesto":

"Just as the technology of printing altered and reduced the power of 
medieval guilds and the social power structure, so too will 
cryptologic methods fundamentally alter the nature of corporations 
and of government interference in economic transactions. Combined 
with emerging information markets, crypto anarchy will create a 
liquid market for any and all material which can be put into words 
and pictures. And just as a seemingly minor invention like barbed 
wire made possible the fencing-off of vast ranches and farms, thus 
altering forever the concepts of land and property rights in the 
frontier West, so too will the seemingly minor discovery out of an 
arcane branch of mathematics come to be the wire clippers which 
dismantle the barbed wire around intellectual property.

Arise, you have nothing to lose but your barbed wire fences!"

I guess that even in 1988, when I dashed off this manifesto, I was
thinking about this kind of twilight of intellectual property as we
know it today.

"Free the bits!"


-Tim May

(P.S. Dean Tribble has rejoined the list. I should point out that, in
my opinion, some of the billing schemes considered for the Xanadu
hypertext system will collide head on with these crypto anarchy
methods for widely disseminating information.)


