Date: Wed, 27 Jan 93 08:50:32 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: ANARCHY: The Twilight of Intellectual Property?

I note that Tim May's recent post about the impact of technology on 
intellectual property rights appears to apply only to copyrights.

Granted, his comments about medieval guilds could be said to regard the
impact of the free exchange of information on what was once analogous to
patents.  I think a better analogy would be to trade secrets, however.  And
in any case Tim does not seem to be saying that *current* technological
developments are putting patents at risk.  Instead, he appears to take aim
only at copyrights.

>Fast forward to today---music and video is barely protectable,
>pirating is commonplace, nearly everyone uses Xerox machines to copy
>whatever they want, bootleg software is passed around, and on and on.
>The courts are choking with lawsuits, countersuits, and chaos.
>
>And newer technology (I mentioned scanners, OCR software, and
>anonymous remailers as one example) is making the protection of
>intellectual property even more tenuous. Unlike land rights, which
>seem fairly defendable (practically, physically), many types of modern
>"intellectual property" have no reasonable defense method short of
>using various Big Brother techniques: . . .

Tim's comments are clearly directed at copyrights (though you could argue
that software copying threatens what few software patents there are).  But
while Tim's comments about the erosion of copyrights deserves our attention
and critical scrutiny, they do not appear to aim at *all* of intellectual
property.  What about other forms of intellectual property?  What about
patents, trademarks, and trade secrets?

I don't foresee the new technologies described by Tim as having an adverse
affect on trademarks.  That's not to say that some other technologies
wouldn't, however.  Technologies that make it easier to copy product
designs would adversely affect a few trademark rights.  See, for example,
the *Sears*, *Compco*, *Bonito Boats* line of cases.  *Bonito* saw the
Supreme Court invalidate a Florida statute that forbade the duplication of
boat hull designs by plug molding, a process that makes it easy to free
ride off another's engineering efforts.  *Bonito* thus left these hull
designs unprotected, as they could not be patented.  There are even newer
technologies that extend this sort of copying to other goods.  But we
shouldn't fool ourselves that the *Bonito* problem will affect many
trademarks; most will remain protected despite the advent of perfect
copying.

The increasing ease with which information can be transferred may adversely
impact trade secrets, but only insofar as it allows people to violate their
contractual agreements not to disclose confidential information.  Computer
technology can likewise aid theives and murderers who need to conspire in
private.  I leave working out the moral implications as an exercise to the
reader.

Lastly, we must consider the impact of new technologies on patent law. 
Here, again, I fail to see that there will be big changes soon.  Rather few
patents protect purely information-based goods, such as algorithms.  I fail
to see how the technologies Tim describes will vastly change patents in
more concrete things like widgets and doo-dads.  I note, though, that
*Bonito*-type copying techniques might.  Why?  It is easier to enforce
patent laws against people who have to invest a great deal of time and
effort in reverse engineering a good and setting up a production line. 
These sort of commonplace patent infringers risk a great deal if they are
caught and shut down.  But because it would vastly lower the costs of
copying and producing patented goods, *Bonito*-type copying would
presumably increase the incentives to infringe on patent rights.  The
advent of intelligent nanotechnology would extend this sort of copying to
complex goods, and thus might have a noticable impact on patent law.



